 \maketitle
 
 \begin{abstract}
-Sequential approximate Bayesian computation (ABC) advances in generations, and a generation cannot end before its slowest simulation. On a parallel machine this barrier idles every worker that finishes early, and for identically distributed simulation times it costs the straggler factor of the workload: the expected maximum of $W$ times over their mean. We introduce a generation-free ABC sampler. It refits its proposal after every arriving evaluation from the best particles evaluated so far, weights each particle against the mixture of proposals it could have come from, and estimates the posterior afterwards from everything that was simulated. We prove the estimator consistent up to a ratio that measures how faithfully its denominator represents the proposals used, and find it calibrated where a pyABC baseline with the same kernel and schedule under-covers. The barrier's cost, predicted from the asynchronous run's timing alone, matches the same sampler run with a barrier over measured costs from $1.2\times$ to $400\times$. On a Cellular Potts tissue simulator the asynchronous sampler completes $2.1$--$2.4\times$ the simulations of pyABC in the same hour and ends at a $4.1\times$ tighter tolerance. The advantage turns on at a few milliseconds per simulation. With the starting bandwidth set from the prior-predictive discrepancies, the method recovers a two-parameter tissue posterior tighter than pyABC's on a larger effective sample, and that effective sample size, a few multiples of the archive size, is the remaining limit.
