 Figure~\ref{fig:algorithm-strip} sets the method beside one generation of ABC-PMC. ABC-PMC proposes a population from one mixture, simulates it, waits for the last simulation, and only then forms the next proposal (row a). Our sampler has no population and no generation (row b). Every evaluation that completes is appended to a growing history (step 1). The $k$ particles with the smallest discrepancies form the \emph{archive}, and the bandwidth of the smooth ABC kernel is tightened as the archive improves (step 2). A Gaussian mixture centered on the archive members, each weighted by its fit at the current bandwidth, is the proposal (steps 3 and 4). One candidate is drawn from it and handed to the worker that has just become free (step 5). By the time that candidate's result arrives the proposal has changed, so the candidate is weighted against the mixture of proposals it could have come from; this is the balance heuristic of AMIS (\S\ref{sec:background}) applied one draw at a time (step 6). The posterior is not read off a final population: after the run, every evaluated particle is reweighted against the proposals that were in use over the whole run.
 
