 \subsection{Metrics}\label{sec:experiments-metrics}
 \paragraph{Tolerance at a matched budget.} To compare samplers on one footing we use the $k$-th order statistic of a method's own discrepancies after $n$ simulations, $\epsilon_{(k)}(n)$: the tolerance at which exactly $k$ of its draws would be accepted. It needs no reference posterior, it does not depend on the bandwidth a schedule happened to report at, and it is what rejection ABC reports at by construction. The \emph{per-simulation efficiency} of the asynchronous sampler over pyABC is the ratio of their $\epsilon_{(k)}$ at the same $n$, above one when the asynchronous tolerance is the tighter; the \emph{equal-wall-clock} ratio is the same quantity at the end of both runs, and the two differ by the throughput ratio. The slope of $\epsilon_{(k)}(n)$ in $\log n$ shows whether more simulations keep converting into a tighter tolerance. We use $k=100$ throughout, over completed simulations only, ordered by completion time (Appendix~\ref{app:protocol}).
