 \section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}
 A generation barrier costs the ratio of the barrier-free throughput to what a generation allows, and that cost can be predicted from the asynchronous run's timing alone: the prediction matched a barrierized twin of our sampler on synthetic runtime laws and on a real tissue simulator. The generation-free sampler we propose removes the barrier and, wherever a simulation costs more than a few milliseconds, converts the simulations it gains into a tighter tolerance; on the tissue simulator that is $2.4\times$ the simulations of pyABC in the same hour and a $4.1\times$ tighter tolerance. Its estimator is consistent up to a partly measured fidelity ratio, calibrated in one dimension and conservative in four, and replayable from the log. Two settings bound what it delivers: the starting bandwidth, which must be set from the prior-predictive scale, and the archive size, which sets the effective sample size.
