 \subsection{Strong scaling on a cheap simulator}
-Lotka--Volterra is an adversarial simulator for a scaling study. Each evaluation takes ${\approx}4$\,ms, so there is little per-evaluation compute to distribute, and an instrumented run (Fig.~\ref{fig:lv-timing}) shows a worker inside the simulator ${\approx}25\%$ of its time at the $48$-worker throughput peak, falling to ${\approx}8\%$ and ${\approx}2\%$ as the job spreads from one node to three to six. The mechanism is the method's single shared population: every completed evaluation is exchanged with all workers so that each can propose from the current archive, an all-to-all exchange whose per-arrival cost grows with the worker count. Aggregate throughput therefore rises almost linearly while the job fits on one node and then declines across fully packed multi-node jobs (Table~\ref{tab:scaling}), because adding ranks beyond one node lengthens the exchange faster than it adds useful work. pyABC is given a population equal to the worker count at high core counts (a smaller population cannot occupy every core); it scales steadily and overtakes the asynchronous sampler beyond three nodes. This is the boundary of the asynchronous advantage on a cheap, homogeneous simulator, and it lifts once the simulator carries real per-evaluation cost (Fig.~\ref{fig:scaling}, right). The archive size moves the boundary, because the per-arrival proposal reconstruction is $\mathcal{O}(k)$. Raising $k$ to $1000$ costs $39\%$ of the single-node peak throughput. Sweeping $k$ over $\{50,100,200,400,800,1000\}$ resolves that cost into a smooth monotone decline, with a single step in the crossover: from beyond three nodes to three at $k\ge400$ (Table~\ref{tab:k-frontier}).
