 \subsection{Calibration against the archive size and the snapshot buffer}
 The calibration tests of \S\ref{sec:results-posterior} fix $k$ and $S$ per benchmark, which leaves open whether the two efficiency knobs must be retuned as the dimension grows. To test this we sweep them directly on a $d$-dimensional Gaussian-mean target, $y_i\sim\mathcal N(\theta,\sigma^2 I_d)$ with $\theta\in\mathbb R^d$ under a flat box prior, whose posterior is known exactly at every $d$ (SBC against that exact posterior returns $0.496/0.794/0.905/0.952$, so the target itself is calibrated and any departure below is a property of the method). The grid is $d\in\{1,2,4,8,16\}\times k\in\{50,100,200,400,800\}\times S\in\{0,5,20,50\}$, $1000$ trials per cell, reporting the same full-history estimator (Table~\ref{tab:ks-sweep}). $S$ sets both the online buffer length and the number of reconstructed proposals in the posterior denominator (\S\ref{sec:method-amis}), and the sweep varies the two together; at $S=0$ the denominator holds only the first and the last reconstructed proposal, so the $S=0$ column tests the posterior-side mixture as much as the online buffer. At $S=0$ the estimator is over-confident in one and two dimensions (mean deviation $-0.088$); every $S\ge5$ removes that deviation, and the experiments use $S=20$.
 
-\paragraph{The archive size does not, in this range, trade calibration against throughput.} Because $k$ has both a statistical effect (above) and a systems cost (per-arrival proposal reconstruction is $\mathcal{O}(k)$, so a larger archive is slower at every worker count; \S\ref{sec:results-boundary}), one might expect to trade throughput for calibration. Over the range we can test, that expectation does not hold. Table~\ref{tab:k-frontier} puts the throughput cost of $k$ next to its worst-case miscalibration over the dimensions swept, using a throughput grid matched on everything but $k$ (Lotka--Volterra, $180$\,s wall cap, five replicates, worker counts $1$--$288$: $k\in\{100,1000\}$ from the main scaling run and $k\in\{50,200,400,800\}$ from a dedicated $k$-frontier run sharing that configuration). Of the six archive sizes, only two are Pareto-optimal, and they are the two smallest: $k=100$ is simultaneously the best-calibrated setting tested (worst deviation $0.064$, at $d=4$) and within $7\%$ of the fastest ($6240$ vs $6684$ simulations/s at the single-node peak). Every larger archive is dominated by it on \emph{both} axes: $k=200$ is $5\%$ slower and nearly three times worse calibrated ($0.180$), $k=400$ is $14\%$ slower and $0.195$, and $k=800$ costs $31\%$ of throughput and is still slightly worse calibrated than $k=100$ ($0.074$). The only faster archive, $k=50$, is the one that fails calibration ($0.250$ at $d=8$). The $k$ decision is therefore a floor to clear: go far enough above the small-archive failure region and stop, since further enlargement adds no calibration and costs throughput monotonically.
